1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,960
And then it turns out, wow, I made 20 predictions.

2
00:00:02,960 --> 00:00:04,080
I was right on all of them.

3
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:06,420
Of course, they were all like whether Norway would invade

4
00:00:06,420 --> 00:00:08,800
Sweden.

5
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:10,920
So this is one where it's better if you

6
00:00:10,920 --> 00:00:13,760
go and find someone who disagrees with you

7
00:00:13,760 --> 00:00:15,440
and is willing to agree to some terms.

8
00:00:15,440 --> 00:00:17,560
And then you get another human being

9
00:00:17,560 --> 00:00:20,280
on the opposite side who is there

10
00:00:20,280 --> 00:00:22,560
to make sure that you don't just scoop up

11
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:25,200
all the easy bets for yourself or all the easy predictions

12
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:27,400
for yourself.

13
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:31,840
Or like another application of rational explanations.

14
00:00:31,840 --> 00:00:36,400
So economist Donald Whitman considers this old argument

15
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:38,400
that there's a lot of wasteful government spending

16
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:39,880
because voters are ignorant.

17
00:00:39,880 --> 00:00:41,360
And he says, well, the fact that they're ignorant

18
00:00:41,360 --> 00:00:42,840
doesn't mean that they underestimate

19
00:00:42,840 --> 00:00:44,960
how much wasteful government spending there is.

20
00:00:44,960 --> 00:00:47,040
They could just as easily overestimate it.

21
00:00:47,040 --> 00:00:49,560
And unless we have some reason otherwise,

22
00:00:49,560 --> 00:00:52,600
why not just figure rational explanations will hold?

23
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:54,280
And some people are overestimating

24
00:00:54,280 --> 00:00:55,700
the wasteful government spending.

25
00:00:55,700 --> 00:00:56,960
Some are underestimating.

26
00:00:56,960 --> 00:00:58,440
But it isn't a general explanation

27
00:00:58,440 --> 00:01:00,240
for why there'd be government waste.

28
00:01:03,240 --> 00:01:07,240
Now, how would you test for any of this in the real world?

29
00:01:07,240 --> 00:01:11,320
So rational explanations first became big in macro

30
00:01:11,320 --> 00:01:15,200
in the 1970s when Keynesian economists hit a log.

31
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:17,360
They had a lot of egg on their face for making

32
00:01:17,360 --> 00:01:19,560
a bunch of bad predictions.

33
00:01:19,560 --> 00:01:22,040
And this is when Robert Lucas and some other people

34
00:01:22,040 --> 00:01:25,000
came along and started pushing another model.

35
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:27,600
Actually, they were borrowing from an earlier economist,

36
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:28,720
John Muth.

37
00:01:28,720 --> 00:01:31,640
But anyway, there were a bunch of empirical tests.

38
00:01:31,640 --> 00:01:34,680
They started performing on things like inflation forecasts

39
00:01:34,680 --> 00:01:37,120
to test them for rational explanations.

40
00:01:37,120 --> 00:01:39,720
So how would you do this?

41
00:01:39,720 --> 00:01:42,120
Well, in order to test for the rational explanations

42
00:01:42,120 --> 00:01:44,720
of inflation forecasts, you need two things.

43
00:01:44,720 --> 00:01:47,440
You need inflation forecasts, and you

44
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:49,680
need actual inflation numbers.

45
00:01:49,680 --> 00:01:52,360
So you need two different things.

46
00:01:52,360 --> 00:01:55,320
And then what people started doing was just

47
00:01:55,320 --> 00:02:00,720
try regressing forecasts on a constant plus a coefficient

48
00:02:00,720 --> 00:02:05,120
times i, where i is actual observed inflation.

49
00:02:05,120 --> 00:02:08,000
So notice, this would be like the forecast for 93

50
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:12,480
being regressed on the actual observed inflation in 1993.

51
00:02:12,480 --> 00:02:15,680
The forecast, of course, come before 1993,

52
00:02:15,680 --> 00:02:17,080
by whatever happened in 1973.

53
00:02:17,080 --> 00:02:19,360
But in the regression, we go and take

54
00:02:19,360 --> 00:02:22,240
the forecast for the year in question.

55
00:02:22,240 --> 00:02:25,120
And then compare them to what actually happened.

56
00:02:25,120 --> 00:02:27,880
And rational explanations implies two things,

57
00:02:27,880 --> 00:02:31,120
alpha equals 0 and beta equals 1.

58
00:02:31,120 --> 00:02:32,960
If you've ever done an hypothesis test where

59
00:02:32,960 --> 00:02:35,600
you have two conditions that you're testing,

60
00:02:35,600 --> 00:02:37,600
that's what they were doing.

61
00:02:37,600 --> 00:02:41,880
An F test that you have to do if you've forgotten F tests.

62
00:02:41,880 --> 00:02:44,400
And basically, what this means is that, first of all,

63
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:48,640
on average, the forecast equals the truth.

64
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:50,680
And then furthermore, if the truth goes up

65
00:02:50,680 --> 00:02:52,560
by 1 percentage point, the forecast goes up

66
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:53,960
by 1 percentage point.

67
00:02:53,960 --> 00:02:55,800
So if there were a positive value of alpha,

68
00:02:55,800 --> 00:02:58,520
this would mean that would mean there's a general tendency

69
00:02:58,520 --> 00:03:00,040
to overestimate.

70
00:03:00,040 --> 00:03:02,560
Negative alpha, general tendency to underestimate.

71
00:03:02,560 --> 00:03:04,720
And then again, if beta were greater than 1,

72
00:03:04,720 --> 00:03:06,560
this would mean that when inflation goes up

73
00:03:06,560 --> 00:03:08,240
by a point, by 1 percentage point,

74
00:03:08,240 --> 00:03:11,040
forecasts are going up by more than that.

75
00:03:11,040 --> 00:03:15,040
So that's what the rational expectations forecast would be.

76
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:19,600
And you could imagine doing any number of macroeconomic

77
00:03:19,600 --> 00:03:23,560
or other forecasts and testing for rational expectations.

78
00:03:30,280 --> 00:03:32,640
Now, something that's been very interesting to me

79
00:03:32,640 --> 00:03:35,520
is testing economic beliefs themselves

80
00:03:35,520 --> 00:03:37,440
for rational expectations.

81
00:03:37,440 --> 00:03:39,880
Because at least when I got into economics,

82
00:03:39,880 --> 00:03:42,200
the reason why I liked it is because the economists

83
00:03:42,200 --> 00:03:44,880
were telling me the opposite of what other everyone else had

84
00:03:44,880 --> 00:03:46,000
told me.

85
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:47,360
That's why I liked it.

86
00:03:47,360 --> 00:03:48,840
So I liked economists saying, you

87
00:03:48,840 --> 00:03:51,600
know all the stuff that teachers and parents and media tell you?

88
00:03:51,600 --> 00:03:53,080
Wrong.

89
00:03:53,080 --> 00:03:55,880
And not just sometimes too high, sometimes too low,

90
00:03:55,880 --> 00:03:57,360
but predictably wrong.

91
00:03:57,360 --> 00:03:59,480
Like the economist saying, you know

92
00:03:59,480 --> 00:04:01,800
all this complaining about free trade you've heard?

93
00:04:01,800 --> 00:04:02,300
Wrong.

94
00:04:02,300 --> 00:04:04,560
Free trade's great.

95
00:04:04,560 --> 00:04:08,920
Or you know all this stuff about how the more regulation

96
00:04:08,920 --> 00:04:10,040
of drugs, the better?

97
00:04:10,040 --> 00:04:10,680
Wrong.

98
00:04:10,680 --> 00:04:12,480
Regulation of drugs can kill people.

99
00:04:12,480 --> 00:04:14,280
Here's how.

100
00:04:14,280 --> 00:04:17,720
You're murdering people with regulation.

101
00:04:17,720 --> 00:04:19,640
Negligent manslaughter.

102
00:04:19,640 --> 00:04:21,560
Anyway.

103
00:04:21,560 --> 00:04:24,560
Or you know how everyone's always

104
00:04:24,560 --> 00:04:27,600
talking about the disemployment effect of the minimum wage?

105
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:28,200
No.

106
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:30,200
Exactly.

107
00:04:30,200 --> 00:04:31,840
They should be.

108
00:04:31,840 --> 00:04:34,360
They're acting like there's no disemployment effect.

109
00:04:34,360 --> 00:04:36,800
But there's got to be a disemployment effect.

110
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:40,960
A minimum wage is $1 million an hour.

111
00:04:40,960 --> 00:04:43,560
So anyway, that's what got me interested in economics

112
00:04:43,560 --> 00:04:46,680
was economists going and saying, these

113
00:04:46,680 --> 00:04:51,440
are the results of our study or our work, which

114
00:04:51,440 --> 00:04:54,200
can paint a very different picture than the regular one

115
00:04:54,200 --> 00:04:57,480
in a very predictable direction.

116
00:04:57,480 --> 00:04:59,680
So anyway, this is why I got excited.

117
00:04:59,680 --> 00:05:02,000
Now the fact that it excited me doesn't mean it's true.

118
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:03,280
Unfortunately.

119
00:05:03,280 --> 00:05:05,680
The world should work that way, but it does not

120
00:05:05,680 --> 00:05:07,680
have to work that way.

121
00:05:07,680 --> 00:05:09,280
And when I got into grad school and I

122
00:05:09,280 --> 00:05:11,880
tried making any of the arguments that got me

123
00:05:11,880 --> 00:05:14,480
interested in economics, normally people would just say,

124
00:05:14,480 --> 00:05:16,680
now this.

125
00:05:16,680 --> 00:05:18,720
So you can't say that people underestimate

126
00:05:18,720 --> 00:05:20,400
the benefits of free trade.

127
00:05:20,400 --> 00:05:23,240
No, you must assume that on average they're correct.

128
00:05:23,240 --> 00:05:25,360
All you can say is maybe sometimes they

129
00:05:25,360 --> 00:05:27,400
might be too high, sometimes they may be too low.

130
00:05:27,400 --> 00:05:30,520
That's all you're allowed to say in a rational explanations

131
00:05:30,520 --> 00:05:31,020
framework.

132
00:05:31,020 --> 00:05:32,800
But maybe rational explanations is wrong.

133
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:37,760
All kinds.

134
00:05:37,760 --> 00:05:42,800
Anyway, so when I started working on this,

135
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:45,320
I expected there to be at least some decent empirics.

136
00:05:45,320 --> 00:05:47,720
Really, a lot of cases there's none.

137
00:05:47,720 --> 00:05:50,760
And this is more of just a convenient assumption.

138
00:05:50,760 --> 00:05:53,040
And it can be convenient in two ways.

139
00:05:53,040 --> 00:05:54,920
One is it's just empirically convenient

140
00:05:54,920 --> 00:05:56,920
because if this is the way the whole world works,

141
00:05:56,920 --> 00:05:59,440
there's so many questions you don't have to study anymore.

142
00:05:59,440 --> 00:06:01,280
So I went into it like, well, let's just

143
00:06:01,280 --> 00:06:03,400
find what the truth is, and then we know on average

144
00:06:03,400 --> 00:06:05,080
that's what everybody believes.

145
00:06:05,080 --> 00:06:08,160
So that simplifies your life a lot.

146
00:06:08,160 --> 00:06:09,720
And then of course, if you just like

147
00:06:09,720 --> 00:06:12,320
to think the world works fine the way that it is,

148
00:06:12,320 --> 00:06:14,280
this is also very politically convenient.

149
00:06:14,280 --> 00:06:17,160
So you just want to put your blessing on the status quo,

150
00:06:17,160 --> 00:06:19,480
then very naturally something like this

151
00:06:19,480 --> 00:06:20,560
is going to appeal to you.

152
00:06:20,560 --> 00:06:23,720
But I say, look, if most people think that education is good,

153
00:06:23,720 --> 00:06:26,000
that means it's good.

154
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:29,240
And if you disagree, then almost certainly you're wrong.

155
00:06:29,240 --> 00:06:31,160
It's like, what are the odds that you're right

156
00:06:31,160 --> 00:06:32,560
and everyone else is wrong?

157
00:06:32,560 --> 00:06:34,080
It's like, oh, man.

158
00:06:34,080 --> 00:06:36,360
If that's where I ran, I would never have gone into economics

159
00:06:36,360 --> 00:06:37,040
if that's what I thought.

160
00:06:37,040 --> 00:06:38,680
I would have found some other area where most people were

161
00:06:38,680 --> 00:06:39,680
screwed out then.

162
00:06:39,680 --> 00:06:41,240
Would have moved into that.

163
00:06:41,240 --> 00:06:44,840
But anyway, so this is what I was dealing with.

164
00:06:44,840 --> 00:06:47,200
So one of the first projects in the undergraduate school

165
00:06:47,200 --> 00:06:49,800
was actually trying to get some data on this.

166
00:06:49,800 --> 00:06:51,440
And it turned out some other people

167
00:06:51,440 --> 00:06:53,840
had already created all the data, which

168
00:06:53,840 --> 00:06:56,400
was a very expensive data set, hundreds of thousands of

169
00:06:56,400 --> 00:06:57,320
dollars.

170
00:06:57,320 --> 00:06:58,760
And then they published one paper

171
00:06:58,760 --> 00:07:01,240
in the Journal of Economic Perspectives using the data,

172
00:07:01,240 --> 00:07:03,160
and then dropped the whole topic.

173
00:07:03,160 --> 00:07:06,200
And when I asked, they just gave me the data for free.

174
00:07:06,200 --> 00:07:09,600
And out of that, I got six papers, a book.

175
00:07:09,600 --> 00:07:13,200
So this is the kind of thing, don't look a gift horse

176
00:07:13,200 --> 00:07:13,760
in the mouth.

177
00:07:13,760 --> 00:07:16,000
Don't say, well, why did you do this whole study

178
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:18,200
if you were barely willing to write one paper on it?

179
00:07:18,200 --> 00:07:20,720
Instead, just say, thank you for sharing.

180
00:07:20,720 --> 00:07:23,680
And a more general lesson, there are actually

181
00:07:23,680 --> 00:07:26,400
a lot of data sets that are out there that

182
00:07:26,400 --> 00:07:28,640
have barely been used.

183
00:07:28,640 --> 00:07:30,920
So for example, there are at least a lot of variables

184
00:07:30,920 --> 00:07:32,160
that have barely been used.

185
00:07:32,160 --> 00:07:34,080
I think it's confusing as to what's going on.

186
00:07:34,080 --> 00:07:37,080
Presumably, people just had more money than they had questions.

187
00:07:37,080 --> 00:07:39,640
So they needed to know about the questions they cared about.

188
00:07:39,640 --> 00:07:41,560
So for example, in the General Social Survey,

189
00:07:41,560 --> 00:07:44,280
if you go look at that, if you go to Google Scholar

190
00:07:44,280 --> 00:07:47,120
and just put in different variable names,

191
00:07:47,120 --> 00:07:49,200
it's got to be a variable name that's distinctive,

192
00:07:49,200 --> 00:07:52,000
like the word help poor, one word.

193
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:52,960
So it's not a lot.

194
00:07:52,960 --> 00:07:55,040
Other than the General Social Survey,

195
00:07:55,040 --> 00:07:57,480
there's not a lot of people who use that word.

196
00:07:57,480 --> 00:07:59,440
In fact, it's probably only there.

197
00:07:59,440 --> 00:08:01,440
If you go to Google Scholar and put that in,

198
00:08:01,440 --> 00:08:03,000
you'll find there are many questions

199
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:05,000
in the General Social Survey that no one has ever

200
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:08,040
written a paper about, ever.

201
00:08:08,040 --> 00:08:12,760
So why shouldn't you be the person that writes that paper?

202
00:08:12,760 --> 00:08:15,600
And no one has ever done this.

203
00:08:15,600 --> 00:08:17,120
And that's original.

204
00:08:17,120 --> 00:08:21,640
If no one's ever done it, that's called original research.

205
00:08:21,640 --> 00:08:25,720
And what rewards do we give out for original research?

206
00:08:25,720 --> 00:08:26,220
Citations.

207
00:08:26,220 --> 00:08:27,560
It's a job for life.

208
00:08:27,560 --> 00:08:28,560
So yes.

209
00:08:28,560 --> 00:08:35,520
So it's all, of course, when you find these 20 door

210
00:08:35,520 --> 00:08:36,920
bills sitting on the sidewalk, you

211
00:08:36,920 --> 00:08:38,520
don't want to make sure are they real.

212
00:08:38,520 --> 00:08:41,280
But if they are, well, great.

213
00:08:41,280 --> 00:08:42,440
Don't say, how can this be?

214
00:08:42,440 --> 00:08:43,440
Go for it.

215
00:08:43,440 --> 00:08:44,840
Anyway.

216
00:08:44,840 --> 00:08:47,240
So there was something called the Survey of Americans

217
00:08:47,240 --> 00:08:49,560
that Consumed the Economy that asked economists

218
00:08:49,560 --> 00:08:52,280
and the general public identical questions about how

219
00:08:52,280 --> 00:08:53,760
the economy works.

220
00:08:53,760 --> 00:08:55,600
And I got this data set.

221
00:08:55,600 --> 00:08:58,600
And then, right setting up tests for rationalizations

222
00:08:58,600 --> 00:09:00,400
for the public's economic beliefs

223
00:09:00,400 --> 00:09:05,240
to go and do some empirical tests on the kinds of stories

224
00:09:05,240 --> 00:09:08,000
that got me interested in economics in the first place.

225
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:09,920
And a very natural way to do it is

226
00:09:09,920 --> 00:09:13,880
you got some measure of the beliefs of the public.

227
00:09:13,880 --> 00:09:15,440
And now, this is the endemic.

228
00:09:15,440 --> 00:09:19,360
So here we actually have forecast and truth.

229
00:09:19,360 --> 00:09:21,920
Now, unfortunately, for how the economy works,

230
00:09:21,920 --> 00:09:26,040
it's hard to get an uncontroversial measure of truth.

231
00:09:26,040 --> 00:09:27,680
So how can you do empirics?

232
00:09:27,680 --> 00:09:29,400
Well, we can do this.

233
00:09:29,400 --> 00:09:30,880
We can at least say, look, according

234
00:09:30,880 --> 00:09:33,600
to rational expectations, laymen and experts

235
00:09:33,600 --> 00:09:35,160
should have the same average belief.

236
00:09:37,720 --> 00:09:40,320
Because all that should be occurring

237
00:09:40,320 --> 00:09:42,480
when people learn more is that it should be compressing

238
00:09:42,480 --> 00:09:45,840
beliefs around the truth without changing the meaning.

239
00:09:45,840 --> 00:09:48,800
So we can go and test rational explanations

240
00:09:48,800 --> 00:09:51,160
by going and getting people's beliefs

241
00:09:51,160 --> 00:09:55,160
and regressing on a constant plus a coefficient times

242
00:09:55,160 --> 00:09:58,160
a dummy variable for whether you're an economist, where

243
00:09:58,160 --> 00:10:05,320
1 equals you are and 0 equals I don't know.

244
00:10:05,320 --> 00:10:07,680
Now, these regressions leave open the possibility

245
00:10:07,680 --> 00:10:10,120
that it's economists who are biased and the general public

246
00:10:10,120 --> 00:10:11,440
that is correct.

247
00:10:11,440 --> 00:10:13,080
So there is that problem.

248
00:10:13,080 --> 00:10:16,280
At least we can say one group is wrong.

249
00:10:16,280 --> 00:10:23,000
And then we can also entertain arguments either way.

250
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:25,400
So there is the argument that when people study a subject

251
00:10:25,400 --> 00:10:27,560
for a long time, they generally know more about it.

252
00:10:27,560 --> 00:10:29,480
It seems like a good starting place.

253
00:10:29,480 --> 00:10:31,640
There are counter-arguments about why experts might be

254
00:10:31,640 --> 00:10:33,040
biased, but that's false.

255
00:10:33,040 --> 00:10:34,880
Yeah, you had a question?

256
00:10:34,880 --> 00:10:36,600
What if both groups are wrong?

257
00:10:36,600 --> 00:10:37,600
Yeah.

258
00:10:37,600 --> 00:10:39,520
Well, so that's another possibility.

259
00:10:39,520 --> 00:10:40,960
So we could just find them both.

260
00:10:40,960 --> 00:10:43,360
So actually, even if they had the same view, it's possible.

261
00:10:43,360 --> 00:10:44,960
It's possible that they're both wrong.

262
00:10:44,960 --> 00:10:48,080
But this is still a test of rationalizations

263
00:10:48,080 --> 00:10:52,000
in that it is possible we'll find one or the other is wrong.

264
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:54,440
But yeah, it could be that they're equal,

265
00:10:54,440 --> 00:10:56,600
and then rationalization still fails.

266
00:10:56,600 --> 00:10:58,240
But yeah, as soon as it's a case where

267
00:10:58,240 --> 00:11:01,400
